\begin{abstract}
Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models built on large vision-language models (VLMs) generalize well semantically, but VLMs are pre-trained on high-level understanding tasks and under-emphasize the low-level spatial cues and physical dynamics that precise manipulation needs. Prediction-oriented pre-training, in contrast, improves spatial grounding but lacks semantic robustness. We propose UP-VLA, a unified autoregressive VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding (MMU) on image-text data and future visual prediction (PRE) on robot data in a single Phi-1.5 backbone, using unified prompting and attention masks, and then conditions action generation on both a generated scene description and predicted future image tokens. On CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, UP-VLA reaches an average length of 4.08 tasks versus 3.06 for GR-1 (about 33\% higher), and 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D. On a real Franka Panda it improves both unseen-object grasping and precision tasks. Ablations show that prediction is crucial for spatial and dynamics grounding while MMU drives generalization to unseen objects.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Large vision-language models (VLMs) provide strong semantic grounding and reasoning, and recent Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models initialize from them to improve open-world generalization in robotics \citep{brohan2023rt,kim24openvla}. However, VLMs are predominantly pre-trained on high-level understanding tasks such as visual question answering, and therefore tend to under-emphasize low-level visual and spatial information (depth, size, relative pose) and physical dynamics. These cues are critical for precise manipulation, long-horizon control and adaptation to new scenes.

Prior work shows a dichotomy (\cref{fig:intro,fig:compare}). VLM-initialized VLAs excel at semantic generalization but struggle with fine-grained control; prediction-based policies pre-trained with future frame or video generation gain spatial grounding and physical consistency but lack semantic robustness \citep{wu2023unleashing,black2023zero}. This motivates a unified paradigm that keeps the strengths of both.

We present UP-VLA, which trains a single autoregressive VLA with two complementary objectives: multi-modal understanding (MMU) from large-scale image-text data and future visual prediction (PRE) from robot video/action data. A unified prompting and attention-masking scheme fuses them so that action generation can condition on both semantic scene descriptions and predicted visual futures. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item UP-VLA, a unified VLA that jointly trains multi-modal understanding and future prediction in an autoregressive framework.
\item A unified prompting and attention masking strategy that mixes continuous MMU tokens and discrete predictive tokens in one LLM.
\item A joint prediction-and-understanding action mechanism conditioning actions on scene descriptions and predicted futures.
\item State-of-the-art results on CALVIN (ABC$\rightarrow$D average length 4.08, about +33\% over GR-1) and real-world gains in both semantic generalization and precision tasks.
\end{itemize}

