\section{Related Works}
\textbf{VLM-based VLAs.} Large VLMs have been adapted for robot control by treating actions as tokens, as in RT-2 \citep{brohan2023rt} and OpenVLA \citep{kim24openvla}, and by imitation-learning heads on VLMs, as in Robo-Flamingo \citep{li2023vision}. 3D-VLA \citep{zhen20243d} adds 3D-aware generative modeling. These models inherit semantic generalization from web-scale pre-training but, as noted above, receive little supervision on low-level spatial and dynamics information.

\textbf{Prediction-based policies.} Another line pre-trains on future frame or video prediction and uses the predicted futures or latent features for control: GR-1 \citep{wu2023unleashing}, SuSIE \citep{black2023zero}, and Uni-Pi \citep{du2023video}; related approaches use joint denoising of vision and action \citep{guo2024prediction}. 3D Diffuser Actor \citep{ke20243d} and Diffusion Policy \citep{chi2023diffusionpolicy} are strong policies without a language-model backbone. Such models improve spatial grounding but lack the semantic robustness of VLMs.

\textbf{Why unify them?} The two families have complementary failure modes. Models initialized from VLMs retain concept-level grounding from image-text data, yet that data rarely emphasizes depth, object size, relative pose or how scenes evolve under actions. Prediction-based models are trained on exactly this physical information through future frame or video generation, but without large-scale language-grounded data they generalize less well to novel objects and instructions. Prior work therefore trades one capability against the other; UP-VLA's goal is to train both in one model rather than choosing.

\textbf{Embodied reasoning with language models.} Language models and VLMs have also been used for planning and embodied reasoning \citep{driess2023palm,mu2024embodiedgpt}, which exploits their semantics but does not by itself supervise low-level dynamics. UP-VLA instead trains the policy backbone with both kinds of signal.

\textbf{Unified understanding and generation.} Recent multi-modal models unify image understanding and generation in a single model \citep{xie2024show,dong2023dreamllm}. UP-VLA brings this idea to embodied agents, unifying understanding and future prediction together with action generation.

\section{Preliminaries}
A VLA policy receives the current observation $O_t$ and a language instruction $L$ and outputs a chunk of low-level actions $a_{t:t+\Delta t}$. We use CLIP-ViT \citep{radford2021learning} as the continuous image encoder, a VQ-GAN tokenizer \citep{esser2021taming} for discrete image tokens, and the Phi-1.5 language model \citep{li2023textbooks} as backbone. All encoders are frozen and the LLM is fully fine-tuned.

\textbf{Problem setting.} Training data consist of image-text pairs for understanding and of robot demonstrations $(O_t, L, a_{t:t+\Delta t}, O_{t+\Delta t})$ for prediction and action. The two data sources differ in what they emphasize: the former in semantics, the latter in low-level spatial detail and physical dynamics. The question we study is how to use both within one policy so that actions benefit from semantic scene comprehension and from explicit modeling of how the scene will change.

\section{Methodology}
\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig3_v3.png}
\caption{Overview of UP-VLA.}
\label{fig:overview}
\end{figure*}

UP-VLA is an autoregressive VLA with a single LLM backbone that performs three functions: multi-modal understanding, future image token prediction, and action generation. It is trained in two stages, pre-training on MMU and PRE data, followed by action finetuning with joint prompts. The design principle is that images enter through two complementary pathways, a continuous one for semantics and a discrete one for prediction, which are fused by prompting and attention masking.

\textbf{Tokenization.} Images are encoded by two pathways (\cref{fig:overview}). For MMU, CLIP-ViT with an MLP projection $E_1$ maps an image into continuous tokens $u$ in the language embedding space. For prediction, the VQ-GAN encoder $E_2$ yields discrete codebook tokens $v$; future prediction operates on token indices and a VQ decoder reconstructs images.

\textbf{Unified prompting and attention masks.} For MMU the input is $\{[\mathrm{MMU}], u, l\}$: image tokens precede text so they can attend to each other, and the LLM predicts next language tokens, $\hat{L}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(I,L)$. For PRE the input is $\{[\mathrm{PRE}], l, v_t\}$: image tokens follow the text so they attend to all preceding tokens, and future image tokens are predicted at aligned positions rather than as next-token over the sequence, $\hat{O}_{t+\Delta t}=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t,L)$ (\cref{fig:attn}). For action learning, both streams are concatenated so actions attend to semantic (MMU) and predictive visual tokens.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fig4.png}
\caption{Illustration of the unified prompting and attention mechanism.}
\label{fig:attn}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Joint prediction-and-understanding for action.} Given $(O_t, L)$, we first generate a scene description $s=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{MMU}}(O_t, L_{\mathrm{prompt}})$ (e.g.\ ``describe this image''), embed additional visual information $E_1(O'_t)$, and form the extended instruction $L'=[E_1(O'_t), s, L]$. The model then jointly predicts future image tokens and actions, $(\hat{O}_{t+\Delta t},\hat{H}^A_{t:t+\Delta t})=\pi_\theta^{\mathrm{PRE}}(O_t, L')$. A lightweight policy head (single-layer attention pooling, MAP, followed by an MLP) maps action tokens to continuous actions: $\hat{a}_{t:t+\Delta t}=\mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{MAP}(\hat{H}^A_{t:t+\Delta t}))$.

\textbf{Objectives.} $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}$ is cross-entropy over next text tokens given image tokens; $\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}$ is cross-entropy over future image tokens, $P(O_{t+\Delta t}|O_t,L)=\prod_{j=1}^{M}p_\theta(v'_j|l,v_{1:M})$; and
$\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}=\|\hat a_{\mathrm{pos}}-a_{\mathrm{pos}}\|_2^2+\mathrm{BCE}(\hat a_{\mathrm{end}},a_{\mathrm{end}})$. The total loss is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}=\lambda_1\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MMU}}+\lambda_2\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PRE}}+\lambda_3\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{ACT}}.
\end{equation}

\textbf{Design rationale.} Placing image tokens before text in MMU lets image tokens attend to one another before language is generated, matching the usual understanding setup. In PRE the image tokens come after the text so each can attend to the instruction and all preceding tokens, and the output at each aligned position is the corresponding future token. Because the action tokens can attend to both the MMU-derived description and the visual tokens, low-level control is conditioned on high-level understanding and on predicted dynamics. Keeping encoders frozen and fully finetuning only the LLM keeps the training recipe simple.

\textbf{Training and inference.} Stage 1 pre-trains on a mixture of MMU data (LLaVA-tuning-665k, \citealp{liu2024visual}) and PRE data (25k Bridge demonstrations, \citealp{walke2023bridgedata}) with task-specific prompts and masks. Stage 2 finetunes for action on downstream robot data with joint prediction-and-understanding prompting, continuing MMU co-training to retain semantic capability. At test time the model generates $s$, forms $L'$, runs the PRE pathway to obtain future tokens and action tokens, decodes actions with MAP+MLP, and repeats autoregressively.

\section{Experiments}
\textbf{Setup.} In simulation we use CALVIN \citep{mees2022calvin} in the ABC$\rightarrow$D and ABCD$\rightarrow$D settings (scenes A--D), reporting the success rate of completing $1$--$5$ chained tasks and the average length. Real-world experiments use a Franka Emika Panda arm with more than 2k demonstrations over 6 skills, evaluating seen tasks, unseen-object grasping, and precise operations (e.g.\ cable routing, small-object grasping), with 20 trials per task (\cref{fig:env}). Baselines are VLA-based (Robo-Flamingo, 3D-VLA, UP-VLA-RT-2, an RT-2-style variant, RT-1) and prediction-based (GR-1, SuSIE, Uni-Pi, 3D Diffuser Actor, Diffusion Policy); UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu is a GR-1-like model with our backbone.

