\textbf{Evaluation protocol and baselines.} CALVIN asks the agent to complete five language-specified tasks chained in sequence in a zero-shot fashion on the evaluation scene; we report the fraction of rollouts completing at least $k$ tasks in a row and the average number completed (Avg.\ Len). In ABC$\rightarrow$D training uses scenes A, B and C and evaluation uses the unseen scene D, testing generalization; in ABCD$\rightarrow$D all four scenes are seen in training. Baselines span VLA-based models (Robo-Flamingo, 3D-VLA, UP-VLA-RT-2, RT-1) and prediction-based ones (GR-1, SuSIE, Uni-Pi), together with 3D Diffuser Actor and Diffusion Policy. Entries marked with an asterisk (Diffusion Policy, UP-VLA-RT-2, UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu) were reproduced by us; the rest are copied from the original papers. UP-VLA-RT-2 follows the RT-2 recipe of adapting a VLM to emit actions directly using our backbone, while UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu mirrors GR-1 with our backbone and no understanding data.

\textbf{CALVIN.} On ABC$\rightarrow$D (\cref{table1}), UP-VLA attains an average length of 4.08, versus 3.06 for GR-1, 3.35 for 3D Diffuser Actor, 2.47 for Robo-Flamingo and 1.44 for UP-VLA-RT-2, i.e.\ about 33\% over GR-1. On ABCD$\rightarrow$D (\cref{table2}) it reaches 4.42 versus 4.21 (GR-1) and 4.09 (Robo-Flamingo). Baseline numbers marked without an asterisk are copied from the original papers.

