\textbf{Summary of findings.} Three observations follow. First, on CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D, which tests generalization to an unseen scene, the gain over prior work is largest: UP-VLA also exceeds the strong 3D Diffuser Actor (3.35) and our own GR-1-like variant with the same backbone, UP-VLA-phi-w/o-mmu (3.13), isolating the contribution of understanding. Second, under the easier ABCD$\rightarrow$D setting where all scenes are seen in training, differences are smaller (4.42 vs.\ 4.21 and 4.09), but UP-VLA still leads in the longer chains, e.g.\ 0.812 success at five consecutive tasks versus 0.731 for GR-1 and 0.660 for Robo-Flamingo. Third, the real-world comparisons mirror the ablations: variants lacking MMU struggle with unseen objects, and variants lacking prediction struggle with precise operations. The RT-2-style variant, which predicts actions without our unified design, reaches only 1.44 on ABC$\rightarrow$D, indicating that merely initializing from a VLM is insufficient.

\textbf{Ablations.} \cref{table:ablate} reports CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D average length and real-world seen/unseen success. Removing prediction is most damaging (4.08$\rightarrow$1.44 on CALVIN), and removing Bridge pre-training lowers it to 2.74, so predictive pre-training matters for spatial and dynamics grounding. Removing MMU leaves seen performance (0.85 vs.\ 0.80) and CALVIN (3.89) nearly unchanged but drops unseen success from 0.58 to 0.20; removing the MMU condition on actions gives 0.50 unseen and 3.99 on CALVIN, so conditioning actions on MMU-derived scene descriptions further helps. Interestingly, removing MMU slightly raises seen real-world success (0.85 vs.\ 0.80), so the benefit of MMU lies in generalization rather than in-distribution performance. Prediction and Bridge pre-training, in contrast, help on seen and unseen tasks alike in the real world (seen 0.65 without either vs.\ 0.80 with both).

