\textbf{Discussion of the ablation design.} Each ablation removes one ingredient while holding the rest fixed. ``w/o Prediction'' removes future prediction, leaving a VLM-style action model; ``w/o Bridge-Pretrain'' keeps prediction but drops predictive pre-training on Bridge; ``w/o MMU'' removes understanding data and corresponds to a GR-1-like model on our backbone; and ``w/o MMU-Condition'' keeps MMU co-training but does not feed the generated scene description into the action prompt. Reading the table by column, CALVIN ABC$\rightarrow$D is most sensitive to prediction and its pre-training (1.44 and 2.74 against 4.08), whereas unseen-object success is most sensitive to MMU (0.20 against 0.58), and the intermediate value for removing only the condition (0.50) shows that part, but not all, of the MMU benefit comes from explicitly conditioning actions on the description. Because real-world results use 20 trials per task, small differences (e.g.\ 0.80 vs.\ 0.85 on seen tasks) should not be over-interpreted.

