\section{Limitations and Future Work}
Generative artifacts such as background color shifts indicate insufficient visual generation pre-training; better tokenizers or diffusion-based modules may help. MMU occasionally misclassifies objects, reflecting backbone and data scale; larger VLMs and richer data could reduce errors. Joint tasks add sequence length and training cost, which parameter-efficient finetuning or structured memory might reduce. Extending to 3D inputs (depth, point clouds), multiple views, or explicit physics modeling is future work.

\textbf{Generation quality.} The background color shifts in predicted images, most visible when moving from the ABC training scenes to the unseen scene D, show that our generative generalization is limited. We attribute this to insufficient visual generation pre-training: the prediction pathway is pre-trained on only 25k Bridge demonstrations, and the discrete tokenizer limits fidelity. Improved tokenizers or diffusion-based modules are natural next steps.

\textbf{Understanding quality.} MMU errors, such as occasional object misidentification in the VQA visualizations (\cref{fig:vis}), are tied to the small Phi-1.5 backbone and the scale of the data. Larger VLMs and richer curated data could reduce them, and would likely strengthen the semantic generalization we observe on unseen objects.

\textbf{Efficiency.} Producing a scene description and future image tokens before actions lengthens sequences and increases training cost relative to a plain VLA. Parameter-efficient finetuning and structured memory are promising ways to improve scalability.

\textbf{Richer inputs.} We use RGB observations only. Extending to 3D inputs such as depth or point clouds, to multiple views, or to explicit physics modeling could further strengthen spatial and dynamics understanding.

\section{Conclusion}
UP-VLA unifies multi-modal understanding and future visual prediction in one autoregressive VLA, and conditions action generation on scene descriptions and predicted futures. It achieves state-of-the-art CALVIN results (4.08 on ABC$\rightarrow$D, 4.42 on ABCD$\rightarrow$D) and improves real-world semantic generalization and precision manipulation, a practical path toward generalist robot policies with balanced high-level and low-level capabilities.

